otations behind  and they were in Russian and included  Russian language settings third the  Russians released all this information  through false personas one called DC  leaks and the other one called gusoff  err 2.0 DC leaks pretended to be an  American and gusoff ur 2.0 pretended to  be a Romanian but both of these accounts  have been linked forensic ly directly  back to the same command and control  servers in Russia that were owned  operated and maintained by the GRU in  Robert Moeller's indictment you can even  see that immediately before a particular  gusoff ur 2.0 posting the GRU officers  back in Russia were googling the exact  same phrases that became part of gusoff  ur 2.0 s message in other words they  would search for a particular English  phrase via Google perhaps because their  English wasn't so good and then that  same phrase would appear in Gusev ur 2.0  tweets the Russians also really  information through WikiLeaks the pseudo  journalistic institution run by  Australian born hacker Julian Assange  all of this evidence and more led the  intelligence community to issue a report  in January 2017 saying that it was the  Russians who were behind the attacks the  Senate Select Committee on Intelligence  in July 2018 issued a bipartisan report  supporting the intelligence community's  review saying it was a sound  intelligence product and adding their  own conclusion that the interference  campaign was personally approved by  President Vladimir Putin special counsel  Moore's indictment was so detailed that  it named the individual Russian  intelligence officials who were behind  the attack  moreover following the indictment the  New York Times reported that the  intelligence community actually had a  human source close to Vladimir Putin who  reported that Putin personally ordered  the hacking of the DNC  with the intent of aiding Donald Trump's  presidential campaign but that was just  the first part of the Russian campaign  the second part was in effect a troll  factory run by a group known as the  internet research agency that had its  headquarters in Saint Petersburg Russia  this was an attempt to create  disinformation campaigns in the social  media space of America in order to  distract disrupt and disable legitimate  communications by American citizens  about the elections one way the campaign  operated was through BOTS as you may  know bots are artificial networks of  fictitious people who post things and  then echo the posts of other BOTS so  they create the false impression that  10,000 people are retweeting or  reposting on social media about a  particular idea or concept in fact none  of them are real people convincing as  BOTS messages can be to the general  public  an analysis of BOTS behavior looks very  different  that of naturally occurring social  networks that involve say your  communication in mind when you follow me  on Twitter or when you follow the great  courses on Twitter those type of network  communications have a certain organic  naturally occurring shape that is  completely different from that of  communications on artificial bot  networks  simply put artificial networks have a  more hierarchical top-down appearance  and Russian bot networks operated  systematically to sow discord let me  give you just one example during the  election Facebook had a web page for a  man named Melvin Reddick  Melvin lived in Harrisburg Pennsylvania  he said he was originally from  Philadelphia PA he studied at the  Indiana University of Pennsylvania  he went to Central High in Philadelphia  and he posted a lot of very  controversial anti-american opinions for  example in one case he accused the  us-led coalition operating in Syria of  conducting chemical weapons attacks  there when we know in fact it was  Syria's Assad regime that was conducting  those attacks so here's the thing Melvin  Reddick didn't exist  his entire Facebook page was a fake the  profile picture of him and of his  daughter was stolen from the profile of  a Brazilian man who lives down in the  Amazon outside the main cities who had  never heard of the American elections  didn't come from Pennsylvania and  doesn't really speak much English at all  he was tracked down by a newspaper when  his picture appeared he disclaimed the  web page and said he had nothing to do  with it  the Russians simply stole his Facebook  profile photo and used it to create a  fake Facebook page sites like redick's  and the rest of the Russian bot Network  propagated false news on a mass scale in  order to stir the political pot one  example this was a report on Breitbart  news in January 2017 that a thousand man  was  mob had attacked police and set  Germany's oldest church on fire the  report complete with pictures and  details was retweeted thousands of times  and propagated throughout the American  news environment yet it was utterly  false no church had been burnt no mob  had existed the picture was actually  from a meeting of Muslim men that it  occurred several days earlier at which  no violence had occurred but he created  anger and confusion which the Russians  evidently believed would help Donald  Trump's cause even though the Russian  interference that I've described wasn't  targeted directly at our voting  infrastructure I'm afraid we can't take  much comfort in that fact the Russians  also probe the vulnerability of our  voting systems to attack we know for  example that they stole at least 75,000  election records from Illinois mr.  molars indictment says it may have been  as many as 500,000 but the number is  uncertain and that's part of the problem  it's bad enough that the Russians have  demonstrated interest in tampering with  our voting systems and that we currently  have no way to prevent it but we can't  even agree on what they've done once  they've done it did Russian interference  in the 2016 elections actually affect  the election outcome you can find plenty  of arguments on both sides of the issue  online if you mean directly manipulating  the vote the answer is probably not but  if you mean influencing the vote the  answer is almost certainly yes to some  degree to me however what matters most  at this point is preventing it from  happening again  so what then should be our response to  begin with we need to recognize that our  election system is critical  infrastructure in much the same way that  our electric grid is in fact today's  election system is a lot like the  electric grid was 10 or 15 years ago  back then we didn't think the grid was  gonna be the target of an attack it was  spread out among many states and  municipalities security systems for it  varied widely and we badly needed to  invest time energy and capital to fix it  by and large we've done so it's time to  do the same thing now for the election  grid here are some thoughts on how first  we need to identify best practices for  election security and incentivize states  to adopt them the most obvious is that  every state should use backup paper  records purely electronic voting means  there's no way not to check whether a  vote has been tampered with after the  fact and that should be unacceptable to  everyone second we need resources any  improvement in these systems is going to  cost a lot of money  many states transition ill-advisedly  from paper to electronic ballots after  the 2000 election and it costs billions  of dollars now it's going to cause  billions more to change back or at least  half way back the 380 million in state  block grants that Congress approved in  March 2018 is a start but much more will  be needed third we need to encourage  state election officials to share threat  and vulnerability information as well as  information about attacks as they happen  in real time it's no good if Florida  stops an attack but doesn't share how  they did it with Georgia fourth and most  challenging ly we need to find a way to  counteract the prevalence of bots on the  web and the spread of false information  I'm not advocating kicking real people  off the network or censoring speech but  I think it ought to be common ground  that fake people don't have real rights  and so companies that manage social  networks like Twitter Facebook Yahoo and  Bing and all the different news  aggregation sites they need to be  courage to identify and eliminate fake  accounts that's a good start we can also  learn a bit from our friends the 2017  German elections came under assault in  much the same way as ours in 2016 with  fake Russian botnet attacks fake sites  fake news organizations but they were  much less effective in Germany why first  all the major political parties agreed  that they would not rely on any leaked  information from cyberattacks they  wouldn't republished it and they  wouldn't reuse it so there was in effect  a treaty among the major parties that  said country / party we're not going to  let the Russians do this to us we're  going to be on the lookout for leaked  information and we're going to make sure  that we check before we retweet second  the German government engaged in what  might be called counter speech it was an  education campaign for the German public  about the signs of fake reporting and  BOTS in short the government tried to  teach citizens how to identify a Russian  influence campaign so is not to be  misled by it now perhaps neither of  Germany's approaches is feasible in  America but even if they aren't they may  point us in useful directions finally we  need states and localities to change the  way they think about the security of  their registration voting and recount  systems today in spite of all the news  about Russian election interference many  states and local election officials  still don't see cyber security as an  important part of what they do they see  registering voters verifying their  identities and managing the vote as  their task  full-stop for example if you go to most  states today and ask who's in charge of  the cyber security of your election  system the answer is there's no single  person in charge  everybody's task  maintaining his or her own database with  no one person responsible for the state  as a whole that needs to change  states need to devise an architecture of  management that places responsibility  for cybersecurity on somebody's  shoulders only once authority is  centralized can effective training and  testing be established to prevent the  kind of thing that happened at the DNC  in the Clinton campaign staff at all of  our voting agencies needs to be trained  to recognize bad links phishing and  other common hacker ploys Russia is the  problem at the present moment but it's  not our only adversary and the world is  full of talented hackers capable of  causing trouble if we want to have a  hope of safeguarding our election system  in the Internet age the kinds of basic  measures that I've just outlined are  quite frankly indispensable it hardly  needs to be said that given the size of  our nation the diversity of its election  systems and the urgency of securing  leadership from the White House would  help in the run-up to the 2018 elections  that leadership has unfortunately been  absent and without it the security of  the November 2018 elections currently  appears to be very much in doubt even if  those elections go smoothly and without  outside interference however our  political system cannot long endure  without an election infrastructure that  the voters can trust our system is built  on the idea that it is we the people who  grant our leaders the power to lead us  with our votes we have a right to know  that our votes have meaning and the only  way we will know that is if the election  results can be proven fair and accurate  and if we are confident that the voters  were not mislead on a mass scale by  false news and false social media  campaigns in the name of democracy we  must demand no  less  [Music]  [Music]


